+## ITEM B — RQ3(c) retried with structural predictors + paired baseline (2026-08-02) [FINDING — REVERSES the earlier null; structure ≫ type, well-powered]
+
+Supersedes PART 3.8 (R²=0.000/0.007) and the PART-3 correction Item 2 (structure R²=0.011). The earlier low
+numbers came from (a) a **mis-scaled propagation proxy** — summed `change_drift_full`, a *relative* drift ~0.008,
+R²≈0.0002 — and (b) a residual built on the band mean. Both are fixed here.
+
+### B.1 — structural predictors, per episode (from the event logs)
+- **mean changed-node degree** (two definitions, B.2 below).
+- **mean 2-hop neighbourhood size**: for each changed node, nodes within 2 hops in that step's logged `pre_edges`
+  (undirected), averaged over the episode's change events.
+- **summed propagation**: per event = full graph-embedding drift minus the node's own contribution, in
+  **consistent absolute units**. `change_drift_full` (relative) and `delta_h_v_norm` (absolute norm) are different
+  scales (means 0.008 vs 0.095, corr 0.61) so the literal subtraction is invalid; instead ‖Δh_G‖ is recovered from
+  `attenuation_ratio_full = ‖Δh_v‖/‖Δh_G‖`, giving **propagation = ‖Δh_G‖ − ‖Δh_v‖ = delta_h_v_norm·(1/att − 1)**,
+  summed per episode. NOTE it is predominantly NEGATIVE (band means −2.5 / −3.5 / −4.6): the pooled graph-embedding
+  change is on average SMALLER than the changed node's own change → **net attenuation, growing with band** (more
+  nodes to average over) — the pooling-attenuation signature, so this predictor is signed "aggregate-minus-own,"
+  negative = attenuation.
+- **type composition**: property fraction = property events / all change events per episode (the type side).
+
+### B.2 — WHICH DEGREE, both named
+- **Runtime visible-graph degree at fire** (logged `changed_node_degree`, `event_graph.jsonl`): R²=**0.0016** pooled
+  vs resid_pair (up to 0.127 within 30-40) — weak.
+- **Scenario knows out-degree** (out_degree of the changed node in `network_SecureBERT.pkl` `knows_graph`, the
+  RQ3-named quantity, evidence_taskY.md 0.1): R²=**0.0000** pooled, 0.004–0.006 within band. **Undefined for 56.4%
+  of changed nodes** (84,028 mapped / 108,621 unmapped) — the majority are synthetic `JoinNode_*` created at
+  runtime, absent from the scenario knows_graph, so scenario knows out-degree cannot even be computed for most
+  change events.
+- **The conclusion rests on NEITHER degree.** Both are the weakest structural predictor under either definition;
+  the structural signal is carried by 2-hop and propagation (below), so the degree-quantity choice is immaterial
+  to the verdict. Neither is described as bare "degree."
+
+### B.3 — outcome variable improved (paired baseline), variance checked
+Residual = **(baseline root-owned − actual root-owned) − mechanical root loss**, where mechanical root loss =
+logged `root_owned_departures` (owned-root nodes forcibly removed by churn). All 117 CX (band,seed,topology) cells
+have a static pair (median 48 static episodes/cell, min 6), so every one of 4,410 CX episodes is pairable.
+- band-mean baseline: residual sd **5.47** (var 29.9)
+- **paired (per seed×topology) baseline: residual sd 4.85 (var 23.5) → 21.5% variance reduction.**
+Material but not dominant (78% of variance is within-cell episode stochasticity), so the earlier null was NOT
+purely a baseline artefact. Regression redone on the paired residual; the structure≫type conclusion holds on BOTH
+baselines (structure R² 0.182 band / 0.155 paired), so it is not baseline-driven.
+
+### B.4 — structure vs type, same episodes (on resid_pair, N=4410)
+| predictor set | R² pooled | 10-15 | 30-40 | 80-100 |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| **STRUCTURE** (deg+2hop+propagation) | **0.155** | 0.148 | 0.365 | 0.331 |
+|   – 2-hop alone | 0.076 | 0.054 | 0.342 | 0.094 |
+|   – propagation alone | 0.079 | 0.097 | 0.104 | 0.091 |
+|   – degree alone (runtime) | 0.002 | 0.024 | 0.127 | 0.027 |
+| **TYPE** (property fraction) | **0.011** | 0.003 | 0.033 | 0.004 |
+| structure + type | 0.158 | — | — | — |
+
+**Structure explains ~14× more of the behavioural residual than type**, and this is NOT a between-band size
+confound: within every band structure R² (0.148 / 0.365 / 0.331) far exceeds type (0.003 / 0.033 / 0.004) — the
+gap is if anything LARGER within band. (The pooled 0.155 is a conservative floor; within-band it's 0.15–0.37.)
+
+### B.5 — power reported with the result
+Detectable R² @80% power, α.05: **~0.0025 (k=3), ~0.0018 (k=1)**. Both structure (0.155) and type (0.011) sit far
+above the floor → **well-powered; this is NOT the underpowered null of PART 3.8.** The near-zero degree R² (0.002)
+is a genuine "degree barely matters," not underpower. Pseudoreplication caveat (4410 episodes in 117 cells): R² is
+descriptive variance-explained, and the within-band consistency across 5 seeds × many topologies (0.15/0.37/0.33)
+shows the effect is pervasive, not one-cell-driven — the analogue of ITEM A's seed-clustering robustness.
+
+### B.6 — verdict: RQ3(c) ANSWERED (not null)
+**Grouping the non-arithmetic loss by STRUCTURE (2-hop neighbourhood, aggregate-vs-own drift/attenuation) explains
+substantially more than grouping by KIND OF CHANGE — ~15% vs ~1% of the episode-level behavioural residual, well
+above the detectable floor, holding within every band.** The per-event cost attribution ruled out at gate N2 is
+NOT needed: episode granularity already resolves structure ≫ type. This reverses the earlier null, which was an
+artefact of a relative-scale propagation proxy and a band-mean baseline, not a real absence of structural signal.
+LIMITATION carried: still converged-smaller vs undertrained-larger (PART 3.1); the within-band strength at 30-40
+(converged) is the cleanest cell.
+
+## RQ1(a) — three checks on found-count vs nominal size (2026-08-02) [FINDING — the coefficient is NEGATIVE; original claim does NOT survive]
+
+Revises 3.7 (loss ~ found-count R²=0.108 vs nominal size R²=0.017, "tracks discovered graph not nominal size").
+Loss definition locked by reproducing 3.7 exactly: **loss = band-static-mean(final_root_owned) − episode
+final_root_owned_count** (score = `final_root_owned_count`; +loss = worse episode). Found-count = `cumulative_discovered`.
+
+### CHECK 1 — THE SIGN (reported first): NEGATIVE
+loss ~ cumulative found-count (original, episode-level, N=4410): **slope = −0.0576, 95% CI [−0.0625, −0.0527]** —
+**NEGATIVE.** More machines found → LOWER loss, the **opposite** of the "more found → more exposure → more to lose"
+hypothesis. This is exactly the confound channel: a bad episode both finds fewer machines AND loses more, forcing a
+negative slope. Per the pre-committed rule, a negative coefficient means the measured relationship runs opposite to
+the hypothesis and cannot be separated from episode quality — not reframed.
+
+### CHECK 2 — field used, and made exogenous
+Field = `cumulative_discovered` (cumulative-ever), quoted from `event_episode.jsonl`; it is contaminated (partly an
+OUTCOME of how the episode went), as is `n_discovered_end`. Exogenous alternative (a) available: all 117 cells
+pairable by seed+topology (ITEM B.3), and the STATIC arm has no change, so its found-count measures agent+topology
+discovery independent of the change arm's outcome.
+- Episode-level, exogenous static found-count: **slope −0.0251 [−0.0304,−0.0198], R²=0.019** — still NEGATIVE, and
+  R² collapses from 0.108 → 0.019, i.e. **most of the apparent found-count signal was contamination.**
+- Cell-level (n=117), exogenous static found-count vs paired disturbance-loss: slope −0.0112 [−0.0203,−0.0021],
+  R²=0.049, p=0.016 — NEGATIVE. (Change-arm/contaminated version: −0.0134, R²=0.066, p=0.005 — also NEGATIVE.)
+
+### CHECK 3 — NESTED test, inference unit = 117 cells (not 4410 episodes)
+| model (cell-level, paired loss) | R² | increment | F, p |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| band identity alone | **0.4351** | — | — |
+| + static (exogenous) found-count | 0.4401 | ΔR²=+0.0050 | F(1,113)=1.01, **p=0.318 → adds NOTHING** |
+| + change-arm (contaminated) found-count | 0.4910 | ΔR²=+0.0559 | F(1,113)=12.41, p=0.0006 → adds, but this IS the contamination |
+
+Within-band correlations (exogenous static found-count vs paired cell loss): 10-15 r=−0.148 (p=0.38), 30-40
+r=−0.019 (p=0.91), 80-100 r=−0.125 (p=0.44) — all NEGATIVE, all non-significant. The pooled result is **between-band
+only.** Note the reframing of 3.7's "nominal only 0.017": at the episode level nominal size looks weak only because
+per-episode loss is ~98% noise; at the **cell level band identity explains 43.5%** of systematic loss — nominal size
+is NOT weak, it captures nearly all the systematic between-band loss, and exogenous discovery adds nothing to it.
+
+### VERDICT (pre-committed NEGATIVE branch)
+**RQ1(a) is NOT answered in the hypothesized direction.** The found-count↔loss coefficient is NEGATIVE (opposite to
+"more found → more to lose") and cannot be separated from episode quality: (1) sign negative; (2) exogenously the
+R² collapses 0.108→0.019 and stays negative; (3) nested at the cell level, exogenous found-count adds nothing over
+band identity (ΔR²=0.005, p=0.32), the only significant increment coming from the contaminated change-arm predictor.
+The original "found-count predicts 6× better than nominal size" was an artefact of (a) pooling episode noise (which
+deflated nominal size's apparent R²) and (b) outcome contamination in the change-arm discovery count. Reported as
+running opposite to the hypothesis, not reframed.
