+## ATTENUATION vs behavioural residual (2026-08-02, exploratory RQ probe) [protective, one-directional; the two-sided hook FALSIFIED]
+Test of "is pooling attenuation protective or blinding?" att_strength (per event, mean) = ‖Δh_v‖−‖Δh_G‖ (>0 =
+pool swallowed the change); outcome = paired behavioural residual (ITEM B).
+- **SIGN: uniformly NEGATIVE (PROTECTIVE) in every band** — r = −0.295 / −0.418 / −0.400 (all p≪1e-14). More
+  attenuation → LOWER behavioural loss. **No blinding regime**: property-heavy vs churn-heavy split stays negative
+  in both (10-15 −0.33/−0.26, 30-40 −0.56/−0.19, 80-100 −0.48/−0.30) — never flips positive. The two-sided
+  protective-AND-blinding tension I hypothesised is **FALSE**; attenuation only protects (more so under churn).
+- **NOT a quiet-episode artifact**: partial r controlling for #events, #leaves, realized graph-movement ‖Δh_G‖
+  = −0.338 / −0.455 / −0.419 (survives, strengthens).
+- **CAVEAT (endogeneity, unresolved)**: att is measured on the same trajectory the agent shaped, so
+  "good episode → stable owned subgraph → high attenuation AND low loss" is not ruled out. Association, not
+  established mechanism. Would need exogenous (static-arm) attenuation as predictor to argue causality.
+- **Overlaps ITEM B** (propagation ≈ −att_strength already predicted the residual). VERDICT: solid one-directional
+  association, but NOT distinction-grade as-is (one-sided + endogenous + overlaps B).
+
+## PERCEPTION-GAP probe (2026-08-02, exploratory RQ) [SEPARABILITY PASSES + survives the endogeneity killer]
+Unifies #1 (content gap: change the observation can't encode) + #2 (timing gap: change seen late). Per episode:
+inv_frac = fraction of fired change events with |change_drift_full|<eps (didn't reach the pool); defer_frac =
+fraction with touched_node_visible==False at fire (not yet seen). Outcome = paired behavioural residual (ITEM B).
+CELL-LEVEL nested (117 cells, the inference unit):
+| model | base R² | +perception-gap R² | ΔR² | F, p |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| over band + churn + n_events (separability) | 0.400 | 0.525 | **+0.125** | F(2,110)=14.5, p<1e-4 |
+| + EXOGENOUS static-arm discovery control | 0.405 | 0.527 | +0.122 | F(2,109)=14.1, p<1e-4 |
+| + ENDOGENOUS change-arm discovery (episode-quality proxy) | 0.488 | 0.566 | **+0.078** | F(2,109)=9.8, p=1e-4 |
+Component increments over band+churn+nev: invisible/content ΔR²=+0.113 (p<1e-4), deferred/timing ΔR²=+0.070
+(p=2e-4), lag/timing +0.058 (p=8e-4, but mean_lag within-band sign is inconsistent −0.20/−0.11/−0.05 — dropped;
+defer_frac is the clean timing measure, positive in all bands +0.29/+0.24/+0.19).
+**KEY:** the gap survives controlling for episode quality (change-arm discovery lifts base 0.40→0.49, yet the gap
+still adds +0.078, p=1e-4). ~40% of the raw effect was episode-quality co-variation; ~60% is independent. So —
+UNLIKE the attenuation probe — this is NOT merely an artifact of bad episodes. Within-band episode-level: content
+r=+0.22/+0.40/+0.27, deferred r=+0.29/+0.24/+0.19 (all p≪1e-6). VERDICT: separable from size, disturbance amount,
+exogenous discoverability, AND episode quality → the strongest RQ candidate; distinction-viable. Residual caveats:
+discovery is an imperfect quality control; content gap mixes encoding-no-ops (exogenous) with unpooled-node changes;
+cross-band training confound still taints the SCALING reading (#3 untested here); depends partly on #4 (does the
+policy use the pooled channel) for the content arm.
+
+### Follow-ups (a) scaling and (b) content decomposition — both DEFLATE the rich framing (2026-08-02)
+**(b) The "content gap" is ~99.6% undiscovered-node blindness, NOT encoder lossiness.** Composition of zero-drift
+fired events: membership_join/leave on invisible (undiscovered) nodes dominate (12384+9450 / 52818+40815 /
+64082+43462), property encoding-no-ops are 14/330/130 = **0.06%/0.35%/0.12%**. So "content" and "timing" are the
+SAME phenomenon — change on nodes the agent hasn't discovered — not two mechanisms. Nested (cell-level, over
+band+churn+nev): endo/undiscovered ΔR²=+0.077 (p=1e-4, credible); deferred/timing ΔR²=+0.070. The exo/encoding-no-op
+fraction shows ΔR²=+0.199 (p<1e-4) DESPITE being 0.1% of events — **implausible as a mechanism (0.3% no-op rate
+cannot cause 2.7 root loss); treated as a SPURIOUS proxy** (likely property-intensity/topology lurking variable),
+NOT leaned on.
+**(a) The perception gap does NOT explain the non-monotone cost.** Band-mean gaps are nearly FLAT (endo 0.68/0.72/0.70,
+defer 0.68/0.74/0.73) while cost swings 8× (behav resid 0.34/2.73/1.38). Adding the gap does NOT flatten the band
+effect: 30-40 dummy +2.03→+1.83 (persists), 80-100 +0.79→+1.08. **BUT #3 is REAL and strengthened:** relative
+residual (resid/baseline) = **0.043 / 0.118 / 0.046** — 30-40 is worst even normalized by stake, so "medium
+networks hardest" is NOT a little-at-stake artifact. Its cause is NOT the perception gap (separate open finding;
+likely the converged-30-40-vs-undertrained-80-100 training confound or a genuine exploration difficulty peak).
+**NET:** the RQ simplifies to ONE clean finding — undiscovered-frontier blindness drives within-band loss, separable
+from size/churn/episode-quality (+0.078) — but the content-vs-timing split collapses (one mechanism), the
+encoder-lossiness angle is negligible, and the gap does not explain the non-monotonicity. #3 stands as a separate,
+real, unexplained anomaly.
+
+## #3 RESOLVED — the "medium networks hardest" peak is a CONQUEST × SCALE artifact, not a real peak (2026-08-02)
+Capability (STATIC arm, owned_ratio = fraction of ownable nodes captured, no disturbance): 10-15 **0.77**, 30-40
+**0.80**, 80-100 **0.47**. 80-100 only captures HALF the network (undertrained/under-capacity); 10-15 and 30-40
+both near-fully conquer. Relative fragility (behavioural residual / static baseline root-owned) per band: 0.029 /
+**0.111** / 0.043.
+**Conquest is EXOGENOUS here (measured on the static/no-disturbance arm), so this is a clean predictor of the change
+arm's fragility.** Cell-level (n=117):
+- `rel ~ log(size) + conquest`: R²=0.376, **coef conquest = +0.309, coef log(size) = +0.054 (both POSITIVE)**.
+  Fragility rises with BOTH how much the agent conquered AND network size.
+- `rel ~ conquest + band dummies`: conquest +0.242; **30-40 dummy +0.072 ≈ 80-100 dummy +0.086** — after controlling
+  conquest the apparent 30-40 peak VANISHES (80-100 is if anything marginally more fragile). The peak was 80-100's
+  conquest deficit suppressing its fragility.
+- High-conquest(>0.55) 80-100 cells (n=2, underpowered): rel 0.060 > the 0.043 band mean, trending toward 30-40's
+  0.111 — directionally consistent with "if 80-100 were fully conquered it would be the MOST fragile."
+**RESOLUTION:** #3 is NOT a genuine "medium hardest" peak — it is an artifact of the largest band being under-conquered.
+The true (conquest-conditional) law is MONOTONE: **fragility increases with conquest and with scale.** The quotable,
+defensible finding is *"the more thoroughly (and the larger the network) the RL attacker has captured, the greater
+the FRACTION of its foothold it loses when the network changes — success is itself a liability; the biggest network
+looks safest only because the agent never finished conquering it."* LIMITATION: the size-at-fixed-conquest slope
+(+0.054) leans on the 10-15→30-40 contrast; nailing it needs a CONVERGED 80-100 (high conquest at large scale),
+which requires training beyond the 15 Aug stop. The conquest→fragility effect (+0.309) is well-supported and exogenous.
